# Task Management System - Monorepo

## Project Overview
Full-stack task management application with .NET backend and React frontend.
This is a technical interview project demonstrating Clean Architecture, TDD, and modern development practices.

## Monorepo Structure
```
.
├── backend/     # .NET 8 API with Clean Architecture
├── frontend/    # React 18 + TypeScript + MUI
├── e2e/         # Cypress E2E tests
└── justfile     # Task runner commands
```

## Important Style Rules

### No Emojis
- DO NOT use emojis anywhere in the project
- This applies to: code, comments, documentation, scripts, markdown files, console output
- Keep all output messages clean and professional
- Use text descriptions instead of emoji symbols

### Code Standards
- Follow Clean Architecture principles
- Write tests first (TDD)
- Keep solutions simple - avoid over-engineering
- Use meaningful names that explain intent
- DTOs for API contracts, never expose entities
- Async/await for all I/O operations

### Documentation
- Keep documentation concise and practical
- No decorative emojis or symbols
- Use plain markdown formatting
- Focus on technical content

## Commands (justfile)

Development:
- `just setup` - First time setup
- `just dev` - Start all services
- `just stop` - Stop all services

Testing:
- `just test` - Run unit tests
- `just e2e` - Run E2E tests
- `just test-all` - Run all tests

Database:
- `just db-up` - Start database
- `just db-down` - Stop database
- `just db-reset` - Reset database

## Technology Stack

Backend:
- .NET 8 / ASP.NET Core
- PostgreSQL with raw ADO.NET (no EF/Dapper)
- JWT authentication
- xUnit for testing

Frontend:
- React 18 + TypeScript
- Material-UI
- React Router
- Axios
- React Hook Form

E2E:
- Cypress
- Isolated test database

## What to Avoid

Never:
- Use emojis in any file
- Use Entity Framework, Dapper, or MediatR
- Over-engineer simple solutions
- Create abstractions before needed
- Put business logic in controllers
- Expose domain entities in API responses

